{
  "dom_id": "step-q117402",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14130861/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$",
